# Clown kit — the POLYGON half of the clown (cap, hair tufts, ruff, striped
# tunic, stockings, sleeves, mitts, shoes), authored in WAM against clown.blob.
#
# WHY TWO LANGUAGES FOR ONE CHARACTER: see goblin-kit.wam's header — flesh in
# .blob because blended SDFs are round by construction, clothes in WAM because
# smooth-min rounds every edge a garment needs. For THIS character the polygon
# half is also the only way to own COLOUR: all flesh shares one material and
# the face sheet only darkens, so the red nose shell, the purple hair, the
# striped tunic and the red shoes all live here. Attempt one discovered this
# the hard way — its nose was cream like the skin and the owner's verdict was
# "no red nose reading".
#
# THE SKELETON BELOW MIRRORS characters/clown.blob AND MUST TRACK IT. Two
# conversions apply, neither optional:
#
#   * UNITS. WAM lengths are fractions of `height`; .blob's are metres. The
#     clown's height is 1.00, so every number here IS the .blob metre value —
#     but do not let that fool you into skipping the conversion thought if the
#     height ever changes.
#   * PITCH SIGN ON `down` BONES. WAM's pitch tips an `up` bone forward and a
#     `down` bone BACKWARD; .blob's always carries toward +z. Up bones
#     (spine1, chest, neck, skull) share pitch verbatim; down bones (upperarm,
#     forearm, thigh, shin) NEGATE. Silent either way.
#
# FLESH THIS WRAPS, re-marched off the CURRENT clown.blob with sdBody
# bisects at each ring's OWN height (full widths; w = 2 x half-width, d = 2 x
# half-depth; x-scans include the ARMS where they pass, noted). These are the
# numbers the garment margins add to — re-march them if the .blob moves:
#
#   head     y0.55 w=0.210 d=0.163   y0.60 w=0.230 d=0.277   y0.70 w=0.305
#            y0.80 w=0.325 d=0.401   y0.83 w=0.316 d=0.389   y0.86 w=0.301 d=0.370
#            y0.89 w=0.279 d=0.341   y0.92 w=0.248 d=0.301   y0.95 w=0.205 d=0.244
#            y0.98 w=0.137 d=0.155
#   torso    y0.38 w=0.287*          y0.44 w=0.314*          y0.50 w=0.269*
#            y0.52 w=0.246*          y0.56 w=0.203*   (* = arms included in
#            the scan; the trunk alone is ~w 0.13-0.16 under the belly ring)
#   limb     hip ball w=0.092  knee ball w=0.079  shin shaft w=0.051
#            shoulder ball w=0.080  elbow ball w=0.062  hand mitt w=0.072
#
# THE HEAD IS A TALL OVAL NOW (0.33 wide x 0.47 tall against this kit's first
# cut at 0.45 wide): every head-adjacent ring below was re-sized against the
# re-marched numbers, not scaled. Garment margins: +4 mm on the cap band
# (a hat's own grip), +12-16 mm on hanging cloth, +6-8 mm on stockings and
# sleeves.
#
# BUILD:  scripts/build-wam-kit.sh clown
#   -> public/assets/lab/clown-kit.gltf, loaded via KITS in webgpu/lab-main.ts.
#
# EXPECTED LINTS, correct to ignore (same three as the goblin kit): this is
# clothes plus a nose shell, not a whole character, so spine1/chest have no
# direct geometry, nothing reaches the ground plane except the shoe soles,
# and skull-side pieces sit far from their own bone's neighbours by design.
model clown-kit
  height 1.00
  # chunky = 8-sided lofts, matching the goblin kit: faceted cloth against
  # smooth raymarched flesh. On a toy the facets also read as stitching.
  style chunky

# Values are one notch brighter than the hexes read off the reference: the
# lab lights the kit with one narrow key and no LOOK-table env (this task may
# not edit kit-overlay.ts), so flat standard materials render darker than the
# same hex would in WAM's own viewer. First render came back mud.
palette
  royal   #4146ad
  blue    #5a63cc
  pink    #dc97cd
  purple  #9468bf
  grey    #c6c2ba
  red     #e04338
  gold    #e2b13f
  yellow  #f6cf4d
  white   #f6f1e6

skeleton
  # .blob's `root pelvis at 0.36 len=0.06` is a BONE from crotch to hip line;
  # WAM's root is a bare POINT, so `hips` restores the missing segment and
  # every .blob `parent=pelvis` becomes `parent=hips`. See goblin-kit.wam.
  root pelvis at 0.36
  bone hips   parent=pelvis dir=up len=0.06
  bone spine1 parent=hips   dir=up pitch=2   len=0.075
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=2   len=0.075
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-10 len=0.045
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-4  len=0.34
  mirror
    # down-bone pitches NEGATED from .blob: upperarm -8->8, forearm 20->-20,
    # thigh 8->-8, shin -10->10.
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.030 dir=side  len=0.032
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=8   tilt=32 len=0.135
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=-20 tilt=-6 len=0.115
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down             len=0.058
    bone thigh    parent=hips     side=0.044 dir=down pitch=-8 tilt=6  len=0.175
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down pitch=10  tilt=-6 len=0.165
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd len=0.095
  end

parts
  # CAP BAND — the close ring that hugs the crown, OPEN SHELL (cap none).
  #
  # Every piece here is an open shell for the goblin kit's reason: a `cap
  # flat` puts a disc straight through the limb, dead on the bone axis, and
  # the fit test then finds a vertex 5 cm inside the head. Rendered
  # double-sided by kit-overlay.ts.
  #
  # The band is what keeps the cap ON the head while the cone above slumps
  # away behind it. Rings are the head ellipsoid's cross-section at each
  # height PLUS 4 mm (re-marched against the narrowed head; see the table in
  # the header — ring 0 sits at y 0.809 where the scan reads w 0.323 d 0.398,
  # ring 2 at y 0.884 where it reads w 0.285 d 0.354). A TILTED cut across
  # the forehead was tried to match the reference's diagonal cap edge — ray
  # pitched forward, rings sized along it — and floated a centimetre off the
  # ball: an elliptical ring hugs a sphere only in the sphere's own horizontal
  # sections, so horizontal it is. The band is kept SHORT (75 mm) and sits
  # y 0.81-0.88, its bottom edge 63 mm above the eye line (y 0.746) — that
  # margin IS the "cap must not swallow the face" complaint from attempt one.
  loft capband bone=skull at=0.575 dir=up len=0.075 frame=fwd material=royal
    ring 0.00 w=0.330 d=0.405
    ring 0.50 w=0.312 d=0.380
    ring 1.00 w=0.292 d=0.360
    cap start=none end=none

  # CAP CONE — the floppy blue cone, slumping BACKWARD and to one side.
  #
  # THE OWNER'S EXPLICIT COMPLAINT ABOUT ATTEMPT ONE was that its cap
  # swallowed the face. Two things keep that from recurring: the BAND above
  # stops at y 0.884, 63 mm above the eye line (y 0.746), and the CONE is a
  # sweep whose base only just swallows the band's top rim (base ring r
  # 0.150 against the band top's 0.146) — this kit's first cut had a base
  # of 0.205 on a 0.45-wide head, and the front view read as a fat
  # symmetric DOME, not a cone at all.
  #
  # Segments accumulate world-referenced bends (`back=` tips the tangent
  # toward -z, `down=` toward -y): three back-bends carry the cone up and
  # over itself, then down-bends curl the last third past horizontal, which
  # is "the tip droops down behind" in grammar this format actually has.
  # yaw=14 leans the whole cone to one side, like the reference's
  # asymmetric slump; WAM reports the realized path in lint, and the TIP
  # BALL below is placed at the tip that path reports (both references show
  # a small warm ball where the cone ends — chartreuse in one, orange in
  # the other — so the ball is measured, not invented).
  #
  # `cap start=none`: WAM sweeps DEFAULT to a flat start cap — a disc
  # straight through the mount point — which here put a fan dead on the
  # skull-bone axis, 70 mm inside the cranium. Same trap as the goblin
  # kit's `cap flat` note, one syntax earlier.
  # The cone's base ring still deliberately SWALLOWS the band's top rim
  # (see above) — an earlier draft sat the cone on the scalp above the band
  # and the render showed a stripe of bare head between the two pieces.
  sweep capcone bone=skull at=0.797 offset=(0,0.006,-0.008) dir=up pitch=-12 yaw=14 material=royal
    seg len=0.060 r=0.150 back=12
    seg len=0.065 r=0.100 back=16 down=6
    seg len=0.060 r=0.065 back=14 down=18
    seg len=0.055 r=0.038 back=10 down=26
    seg len=0.045 r=0.020 back=6 down=20 tip
    cap start=none

  # TIP BALL — the warm blob at the drooping cone's end, gold. Placed by
  # hand at the tip WAM's lint reports (NOT `on=capcone`: surface snapping
  # sinks a part by a third of its radius and the fit test bounds how deep
  # that may go).
  attach capball bone=skull kind=sphere at=0.95 offset=(-0.070,0.004,-0.227) size=0.026 material=gold

  # BADGE — the little yellow spot where the reference wears its star, high
  # on the cap band above the ear. WAM has no star generator (attach kinds
  # are hoof/box/sphere/eye; lofts make tubes), so this is a round badge at
  # the star's position — same concession attempt one made, kept because the
  # colour note is what the silhouette wants and a bad polygon star would
  # read worse than no star. Placed ABOVE the tuft's sweep volume: an earlier
  # offset at cheek height compiled fine and was entirely inside the hair,
  # which WAM's own lint caught.
  # x pulled in with the narrowed band (0.140 against the band's local
  # half-width 0.150): the badge's inner pole tucks 12 mm into the head —
  # attached — while its face clears the tuft's sweep volume (the first
  # draft's 0.185 sat inside the old fat band).
  attach badge bone=skull kind=sphere at=0.70 offset=(0.140,0.030,0.085) size=0.026 material=yellow

  # HAIR — two wide purple tufts sweeping out from under the cap band,
  # sticking sideways past the cheeks almost like wings. Second-biggest
  # silhouette element after the head itself in the reference, and absent
  # from attempt one entirely.
  #

  # RUFF — grey scallop collar with red pom-poms, UNDER the chin where the
  # reference wears it (bead centres y ~0.556, level with the chest top).
  # This kit's first cut rode the collar at y 0.615, wrapped around the
  # JAW, because the old 0.45-wide head left nowhere else to hang it; the
  # narrowed head (half-width 0.103 at that height) lets it sit at the
  # shoulders like the reference, the head ball nesting INTO it from above.
  # The beads' inner poles (x 0.099) tuck ~6 mm into the head-bottom/chest
  # blend — attached without burying — and their bottoms (y 0.52) rest on
  # the bodice's flared top ring below. Not a torus: WAM has no torus
  # generator, and the scallop IS the look.
  attach bead1 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.133,-0.012,-0.004)         size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead2 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.094,-0.012,0.094)          size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead3 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0,-0.012,0.133)              size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead4 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.094,-0.012,0.094)         size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead5 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.133,-0.012,-0.004)        size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead6 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.094,-0.012,-0.104)        size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead7 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0,-0.012,-0.144)             size=0.034 material=grey
  attach bead8 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.094,-0.012,-0.104)         size=0.034 material=grey
  # Pom-poms: smaller, redder, one notch further OUT (circle 0.178, r 26)
  # and a touch lower, at 22.5-degree offsets between the beads — they
  # stick well past the grey scallops, which is the whole point of them
  # (measured on the reference: poms reach ±0.21 of standing height
  # against the collar's ±0.17). An earlier 0.168 circle lost that race by
  # half a millimetre and the kit test caught it.
  attach pom1 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.164,-0.022,0.069)          size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom2 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.069,-0.022,0.164)          size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom3 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.069,-0.022,0.164)         size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom4 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.164,-0.022,0.069)         size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom5 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.164,-0.022,-0.075)        size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom6 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(-0.069,-0.022,-0.169)        size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom7 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.069,-0.022,-0.169)         size=0.026 material=red
  attach pom8 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.0 offset=(0.164,-0.022,-0.075)         size=0.026 material=red

  # TUNIC BODICE — vertical blue/pink stripes from under the ruff to the
  # hip line. frame=fwd so d spans front-back exactly like the measured
  # flesh depths. The TOP RING FLARES (w 0.230 against the waist rings'
  # 0.16-0.18): the reference's tunic is a stiff bell whose shoulders hold
  # the ruff's beads up (their bottoms land at y 0.52, exactly this ring),
  # with the head ball sitting IN the flare — measured there at ±0.13 of
  # standing height, while the trunk flesh inside is only ±0.06-0.07.
  # Floating cloth by design: the fit test bounds only how deep a garment
  # may sink INTO flesh, and a bell that hugs the belly would read as a
  # sausage casing, not a costume.
  #
  # STRIPES RUN AROUND THE TUBE via material_arc (a constant arc over every
  # ring is a stripe down the length). THE SAMPLING TRAP, diagnosed on
  # attempt one and worth restating: material_arc samples each quad at its
  # ENDING edge angle with INCLUSIVE bounds, and chunky lofts put quad
  # boundaries every 45 degrees — bands authored on the grid (0-45, 90-135,
  # ...) paint EVERY quad one colour and the stripes vanish silently. All
  # four arcs below are offset off the grid by 22 degrees for exactly that
  # reason. Keep them off it.
  loft bodice bone=hips at=0.30 dir=up len=0.1425 frame=fwd material=pink material_arc=blue:22-67,blue:112-157,blue:202-245,blue:292-337
    ring 0.00 w=0.174 d=0.218
    ring 0.33 w=0.178 d=0.216
    ring 0.65 w=0.150 d=0.190
    ring 1.00 w=0.230 d=0.250
    cap start=none end=none

  # TUNIC SKIRT — the bell flare, hip line to the shoe tops. Measured off
  # the reference: the hem lands at y ~0.19, where the shoes begin — the
  # character's legs are STUBS, 45 mm of stocking showing between hem and
  # shoe, and the tunic is nearly half the standing height. This kit's
  # first cut stopped the hem at y 0.27 (mid-thigh, the goblin's fauld
  # lesson applied by reflex) and the render read as long legs in a short
  # shirt — the exact inverse of the reference.
  #
  # Ring sizing measured at each ring's OWN height against the re-marched
  # flesh: at the hem (y 0.195) the splayed knee flesh is ±0.086 and the
  # hem ring's ±0.155 clears it by 69 mm. Attempt one sized its hem off
  # the crotch ring instead and the belly pushed 53 mm through it.
  # Arcs are the BODICE's shifted +45 degrees: the skirt runs dir=down where
  # the bodice runs up the chain, and the arc convention mirrors with the
  # path — unshifted, the two met at the hip with blue facing pink and read
  # as two different garments.
  loft skirt bone=hips at=1.0 dir=down len=0.230 frame=fwd material=pink material_arc=blue:67-112,blue:157-202,blue:247-292,blue:337-382
    ring 0.00 w=0.170 d=0.220
    ring 0.50 w=0.235 d=0.280
    ring 1.00 w=0.310 d=0.330
    cap start=none end=none

  mirror
    # HAIR — two wide purple tufts sweeping out from under the cap band,
    # sticking sideways past the cheeks almost like wings; second-biggest
    # silhouette element after the head itself in the reference, and absent
    # from attempt one entirely. Authored ONCE here, inside the mirror block:
    # the first draft sat outside it and produced ONE wing (the render caught
    # what the compile lint did not).
    #
    # MEASURED OFF THE REFERENCE: the wings span the head's whole height
    # (f 0.05-0.39 of standing height) and reach ±0.25 — on this 1.00 m
    # character that is y 0.64-0.88 reaching x 0.25, a big comma from under
    # the cap band down past the cheek. TWO placement lessons from this
    # pass's renders: roots at y 0.80 left a bare gap beside the cap (the
    # reference's wings start AT the band, f 0.19), and tips beyond ±0.28
    # read as ear-flaps, not wings.
    #
    # Mounted just INSIDE the ball (root x 0.145 against the local
    # half-width 0.162 at y 0.80 — 17 mm buried, attached), aimed dir=side
    # with a slight yaw, then walked down-and-out by four segments: the
    # belly swells to r 0.058 once clear of the ball and the tip tapers as
    # it curls forward at cheek height. The ROOT SEGMENT IS DELIBERATELY
    # THIN (30 mm): a fat ring around an axis below the skin always has
    # vertices deep inside the skull — the fit test rejected a 75 mm-rooted
    # first draft at -71 mm. `cap start=none` for the same reason as the
    # cap cone above (sweeps default to a flat start cap: a disc straight
    # through the mount point).
    sweep tuft bone=skull at=0.78 offset=(0.130,0.000,0.012) dir=side yaw=-14 material=purple
      seg len=0.050 r=0.030 down=34 back=6
      seg len=0.070 r=0.058 down=24
      seg len=0.055 r=0.046 down=20 fwd=8
      seg len=0.038 r=0.020 down=15 tip
      cap start=none

    # THE RED NOSE SHELL — the colour half of the clown's one saturated note.
    # The flesh bead underneath carries the silhouette (see clown.blob); this
    # red button carries the READ. A flattened ellipsoid riding the bead's
    # front like painted latex: w/h/d are FULL extents, so this is 116 mm
    # across by 104 tall by 72 deep, centred 24 mm in front of the bead's
    # centre — its rim stands just proud of the bead's silhouette all the
    # way around while its back plate sits safely inside the flesh. TRACKS
    # THE BEAD: clown.blob moved the bead down to y 0.658 this pass and the
    # shell had to follow by the same 64 mm, or the red lands on the brow.
    attach nose bone=skull kind=sphere at=0.45 offset=(0,-0.109,0.215) size=0.052 w=0.116 h=0.104 d=0.072 material=red

    # STOCKING — thin blue leg, tight (+6-8 mm over the measured balls and
    # shafts), ending just above the ankle where the shoe takes over.
    loft stocking bones=thigh..shin material=blue
      ring 0.08 w=0.098
      ring 0.35 w=0.086
      ring 0.55 w=0.092
      ring 0.75 w=0.074
      ring 0.93 w=0.072
      cap start=none end=none

    # SLEEVE — thin blue arm tube, shoulder to wrist. Slimmer at the top
    # than this kit's first cut (w 0.078, starting at t 0.10): the collar's
    # side beads sit at x ±0.133 and the old 0.090-wide shoulder ring put
    # its outer wall under them — WAM's lint flagged bead5 as entirely
    # inside the sleeve. The reference's sleeves pass UNDER the ruff, thin.
    loft sleeve bones=upperarm..forearm material=blue
      ring 0.10 w=0.078
      ring 0.32 w=0.060
      ring 0.55 w=0.068
      ring 0.80 w=0.056
      ring 0.95 w=0.052
      cap start=none end=none

    # MITT — the white puff. A sphere on the hand bone, sized to swallow
    # the flesh mitten (r 0.036) whole: the glove is one soft shape, no
    # fingers, exactly like the reference. It rides at the skirt wall —
    # cloth contact is unavoidable at chibi proportions and the reference
    # shows the same brush.
    attach mitt bone=hand kind=sphere at=0.60 offset=(0,0,0.005) size=0.044 material=white

    # SHOE — big, bulbous, RED, TOES OUT. Measured on the reference: each
    # shoe is ~0.17 of standing height wide and ~0.16 tall — nearly round
    # in plan — and the toes point OUTWARD (the classic clown splay; the
    # front reference shows the two shoes fanned apart). The .blob's foot
    # bones cannot splay (dir=fwd takes no yaw), so the SHOE does it: this
    # is a free ray off the ankle, and yaw=14 turns it out. That is also
    # what makes 0.15-wide shoes possible at all — feet sit only ±0.048
    # apart, so two unsplayed shoes that wide would overlap each other by
    # 30 mm at the centreline; with the splay the toes clear by ~15 mm.
    # Lofted along the foot ray CONTINUED (shin at=1.0, dir=fwd, longer
    # than the foot bone itself): the foot's toe blob rides past its bone's
    # tail, so a shoe ending at t=1.0 of the foot bone stops short of the
    # toe it exists to exaggerate — the goblin kit's boot lesson verbatim.
    # Oversized IS the joke: 0.16 forward of the ankle plus domes, against
    # a 0.095 foot bone.
    loft shoe bone=shin at=1.0 dir=fwd len=0.160 yaw=14 frame=up material=red
      ring 0.00 w=0.125 d=0.135
      ring 0.30 w=0.155 d=0.145
      ring 0.60 w=0.150 d=0.120
      ring 0.90 w=0.115 d=0.090
      ring 1.00 w=0.075 d=0.060
      cap start=dome end=dome

    # SOLE — the gold underside, a flat box slab hung 62 mm BELOW the ankle
    # axis so it bottoms out at y ~0.010: on the ground, not through it.
    # Sized slightly proud of the red body so a gold rim reads all around,
    # and yawed WITH the shoe (14) so the rim tracks the splayed toe.
    # NOTE it renders matte: shiny materials need an entry in
    # kit-overlay.ts's LOOK table for env reflections, and this task's rules
    # confine edits to characters/ — recorded as a known gap, not fixed by
    # faking emissive.
    loft sole bone=shin at=1.0 dir=fwd len=0.140 yaw=14 offset=(0,-0.062,0.05) frame=up shape=box material=gold
      ring 0.00 w=0.140 d=0.030
      ring 0.50 w=0.160 d=0.028
      ring 1.00 w=0.120 d=0.024
      cap start=none end=none

    # TOE BALL — the small yellow ball on the shoe's toe (both references
    # wear one; ~35 mm across). Hosted on the FOOT bone at its tail (WAM
    # lint flagged the shin-hosted version: its origin sat nearer the
    # foot), placed by hand — NOT `on=shoe`: surface snapping sinks it by a
    # third of its radius, and the fit test bounds how deep that may go.
    # x nudged out (+0.020) so the ball rides the SPLAYED toe the shoe
    # carves, not the unsplayed foot bone under it.
    attach toe bone=foot kind=sphere at=1.0 offset=(0.020,-0.030,0.066) size=0.034 material=yellow
  end

textures
  # Cloth stays matte and quiet — heavy noise would muddy the stripe edges
  # the material_arc work exists to keep crisp. The striped materials take
  # the least of anything.
  texture royal base=#4146ad
    gradient axis=v from=-0.10 to=0.16
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.14
  texture blue base=#5a63cc
    noise scale=0.16 amount=0.04
    ao amount=0.12
  texture pink base=#dc97cd
    noise scale=0.16 amount=0.04
    ao amount=0.12
  texture purple base=#9468bf
    streaks along=v amount=0.08
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.12
  texture grey base=#c6c2ba
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.16
  texture red base=#e04338
    gradient axis=v from=-0.08 to=0.14
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.14
  texture gold base=#e2b13f
    gradient axis=v from=-0.10 to=0.22
    ao amount=0.12
  texture yellow base=#f6cf4d
    ao amount=0.10
  texture white base=#f6f1e6
    ao amount=0.10
